.Dd November 14, 2026
.Dt VIRTIO-NET 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm virtio-net ,
.Nm virtio_net
.Nd driver of the virtio network device
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /sys/class/net/ethN
.Pa /sys/bus/virtio/drivers/virtio_net
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver binds the virtio network device of virtio 1.2, device 1, on the
PCI bus, QEMU's
.Ql -device virtio-net-pci ,
as Linux's virtio_net does, and makes it an Ethernet interface,
.Li ethN .
The device has no registers: a frame is a buffer on a queue shared with
the hypervisor, so a burst of frames costs one notification where an
emulated card costs an exit for each access to a register.
.Pp
The driver takes these features when the device offers them:
.Bl -tag -width "VIRTIO_NET_F_SPEED_DUPLEX"
.It Dv VIRTIO_NET_F_MAC
The device's station address.
A device without it gets a random locally administered address, which
the driver gives the device with
.Dv VIRTIO_NET_F_CTRL_MAC_ADDR .
.It Dv VIRTIO_NET_F_STATUS
The link.
The device interrupts when it changes, and the kernel logs
.Ql virtio_net: link: virtioN: up
or
.Ql down .
A device without it is always up.
.It Dv VIRTIO_NET_F_MTU
The device's largest MTU, which is the interface's MTU and the most it
takes when it is below 1500; one below 68 is ignored.
.It Dv VIRTIO_NET_F_MRG_RXBUF
A frame received may span buffers.
.It Dv VIRTIO_NET_F_CTRL_VQ , Dv VIRTIO_NET_F_CTRL_RX
The control queue, through which the driver asks for every multicast
frame and no promiscuous mode.
.It Dv VIRTIO_NET_F_SPEED_DUPLEX
The link's speed and duplex.
Without it
.Pa /sys/class/net/ethN/speed
reads \-1 and
.Pa duplex
reads
.Ql unknown ,
as on Linux.
.It Dv VIRTIO_F_EVENT_IDX
The device interrupts once for a burst of frames, and is told once of a
burst of buffers.
.El
.Pp
It asks for no checksum or segmentation offload, since the kernel
computes and checks every checksum itself and sends frames of at most
1514 bytes.
The device interrupts by MSI-X, a vector for each queue, or by its
function's line; a device with neither is read at each tick.
.Pp
A write of the function's name,
.Ar 0000:BB:DD.F ,
to
.Pa /sys/bus/pci/drivers/virtio-pci/unbind
stops the device and takes the driver off it, so that
.Xr vfio 4
may have the function.
The interface stays, without a carrier.
A write of the name to
.Pa bind
brings the device up again as the same interface.
.Sh LIMITS
A device of virtio 1.x only: QEMU's
.Ql disable-modern=on
is refused.
One receive and one transmit queue.
An interface is made only at boot: a device bound later that had none is
refused, and an unbound device's interface keeps its addresses, where
Linux removes the interface.
.Pa /sys/class/net/ethN/device
leads to the PCI function, where Linux's leads to the virtio device
below it.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /sys/bus/virtio/drivers/virtio_net
.It Pa /sys/class/net/ethN
The interface's attribute files
.Pq Xr sysfs 5 .
.It Pa /sys/bus/virtio/devices/virtioN
The virtio device, with its
.Pa driver
link while the driver has it.
.It Pa /sys/bus/virtio/drivers/virtio_net
The driver, with a link to each device it has.
.It Pa /sys/bus/pci/drivers/virtio-pci
The transport's bind and unbind.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Boot os-real in QEMU with the device, from rach2/:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ NIC=virtio-net-pci ./run.sh os-real
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr vfio 4 ,
.Xr virtio-gpu 4 ,
.Xr sysfs 5 ,
.Xr ip 8
.Sh HISTORY
The driver follows Linux's drivers/net/virtio_net.c.
Its device is that of virtio 1.2, section 5.1.
